What is QModBus?
----------------

QModBus is a free Qt-based implementation of a ModBus master application.
A graphical user interface allows easy communication with ModBus slaves over
serial line interface. QModBus also includes a bus monitor for sniffing all
traffic on the bus.



Credits
-------

QModBus was originally created by Tobias Junghans / EDC Electronic Design
Chemnitz GmbH (http://www.ed-chemnitz.de), with the Modbus TCP support
designed and implemented by Karl-Heinz Reichel, ing-büro reichel-langer
(http://www.techdrivers.de). See AUTHORS for the full list of contributors.

Upstream project: http://qmodbus.sourceforge.net and
https://github.com/ed-chemnitz/qmodbus

This repository is a Qt6 port of that project.



Requirements
------------

* Qt6 (Core, Gui, Widgets, SerialPort)
* GCC



Building
--------

See INSTALL for information on how to build QModBus.



Usage
-----

QModBus can be launched already connected, instead of always requiring
manual setup in the GUI:

  qmodbus --mode rtu|ascii --serial-port <device> [--baud <rate>]
          [--data-bits 7|8] [--stop-bits 1|1.5|2] [--parity none|odd|even]

  qmodbus --mode tcp --host <ipv4-address> [--tcp-port <port>]

For Modbus TCP, a single request can also be sent automatically right
after connecting:

  qmodbus --mode tcp --host <ipv4-address> [--tcp-port <port>] \
          --slave-id <1-254> --start-address <0-65535> --num-coils <1-65535> \
          --function-code read-coils|read-discrete-inputs|
                          read-holding-registers|read-input-registers

--slave-id, --function-code, --start-address and --num-coils must all be
given together. Only the read function codes are supported here, since
there is no option to supply the value(s) a write would need.

Run `qmodbus --help` for the full list of options.



